  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showers and thunderstorms into tonight can hold \nheavy rain and damaging winds. A small chance of tornadoes and \nhail also exists. Additional storms possible Friday.\n\nWeak wave crossing LE has been touching off a few TSRA farther\nto the west. However, the shallow cu and higher stability\noverhead indicate that it's influence to cause new storms \nlocally will be low. However, the high humidity and slight\nconvergence over nrn tier counties will help a couple of tall\ncells to build up before 22Z. The main show is shortly\nafterward, though. Much stronger short wave trough is moving\nthrough lower MI and should drive a complex of storms through\nmost/all of the CWA this evening. Arrival in the NW should be\naround 22Z and exit to the east of the CWA will be in the \n02-03Z range. The strong dynamics and initial curve to the\nhodographs in the NW may generate a few supercells. Then, the\nvertical wind profile becomes more W-E/linear. That should\nresult in a more clustered or linear mode, ripe for a bow echo.\n70-75F dewpoints and PWAT near 2\" this evening (highest S) will\nmake heavy rain a possibility, too. The risk for flooding still\nexists, especially because of the very wet ground due to the\npast 36-48hr rainfall. However, the faster the storm motions\nare, the lower a risk it could be. The problem this evening is\nas it has been - multiple/repeated storm cores moving over the\nsame spots. Will keep on with the FF Watch thru midnight. Timing\nshould encompass all of the threat.\n\nHeat index numbers haven't quite built to the point expected \nyet, likely due to the delayed onset of sunshine. But, we'll\nkeep the advisory in place until 8 PM as the temps could jump\nanother 5-6F before it starts to cool off over the S now that\nthey have nearly full sunshine.\n\nThe cool air that the storms this evening generate will help to\nnudge a cold front off to our S tonight. But, not very far \nsouth of PA. It won't take much forcing to fire up SCT-NMRS \nstorms on Friday. PoPs may be a little high for Fri-Fri night, \nbut we'll follow the recent trend toward a wetter forecast. \nThese will stay closer to the MD border, but isold storms will \nform as far as the NY border. SPC outlook has been consitently\nworse (SLGT) for the Laurels on Fri. \n\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry weather returns on Saturday and lasts \nthrough the weekend.\n\nUpper low over New England will close even more and drop a\nlittle to the south as cooler air slides into NY/PA. The cold\nfront finally gets far enough to our south to keep all the \nSHRA/TSRA off to our south on Sat. Other than some morning\nvalley fog, the wx will be really nice for the holiday weekend.\nHighs mainly in the 70s and lows 45-55F both Sat night and Sun\nnight. Upper ridge inches to the east from the central states\ninto mid-week. Quiet weather stretch will be welcomed by many.\nNext chc precip isn't until Wed-Thurs.",
